l width the  width of this year's car has increased  from 1,800 millimetres to 2,000  millimetres with a significant amount of  this additional width been allocated to  facilitating wider tires this  dramatically increases the contact area  of the tyres to the ground which  increases the traction the tire has now  if you look at the equations for  friction you will see it as independent  of area friction is simply given as the  phishing of friction multiplied by the  weight of the carrot so how does a wider  tire increase traction a wider contact  area does affect the wear on the tire  meaning a narrower tire will lose its  grip material quicker to counteract that  the tire needs to be made of a harder  material that won't wear as quickly f1  teams have five dry weather materials to  choose from during the race ranging from  ultra soft to hurt the softer materials  have higher coefficient of frictions but  don't last very long so in essence the  wider tires have allowed the softer  higher grip tires to last longer  allowing drivers to brake later and  accelerate harder these wider tires  needed a wider front wing as its primary  purpose is not to generate downforce but  to manipulate the incoming airflow  around the tires the tires can generate  a great deal of drag and also generate a  huge amount of turbulent air which would  prevent the aerodynamic surfaces  downstream of the tire from working  correctly so the front wing is designed  to direct the air to flow around the  tire one of the most beautiful  applications of this airflow management  is this vortex named the Wai 250 vortex  by f1 rules this central portion of the  wing is not allowed to generate any  downforce about 250 millimeters from the  center you may the vortex is a result of  downforce being generated by the front  wing but instead of just being a  negative side effect that causes drag  the vortices can be carefully crafted to  bend the air away from the tires and to  where it's needed to learn more let's  look at some engineering simulations and  compare the differences between the 2016  and 2017 carries these may look scary to  an untrained eye but here is the gist of  what you need to know here we are  looking at air pressure along the car  with the position being indicated here  blue and green is low-pressure air  yellow and red is higher pressure air an  f1 car wants low-pressure air below the  car and high-pressure air above the car  as that is what generates downforce  let's bring the simulation back to the  start and see how these changes have  affected the performance of the car here  you can see the y 250 vortex beginning  to form as air rolls from the upper  surface of the wing to the lower surface  you can clearly see the y 250 vortex is  larger this year as a result of the  larger wing surface and it gets larger  as it travels down the vehicle as the  low-pressure air  in the surrounding air and the Moreira  consumes the less air can get under the  car fills dropping the air pressure you  can see this happening here as the floor  structure begins you can also see the y  250 vortex begins to generate additional  low-pressure vortices this year off the  barge board which steers the vortices  and helps them generate new low-pressure  vortices like this one being formed off  this neat little airfoil the barge board  was not allowed last year in this  location as the area was an exclusion  zone which is an area where structures  are not allowed to be placed teams like  Mercedes have incredibly intricate barge  boards much more complicated than the  ones in this model traveling further we  can see the 2017 version is noticeably  greener here and thus lower pressure  this combined with the wider floor  allows the 2017 car to generate much  more downforce travelling further again  we find the primary cause of this drop  in pressure the diffuser this part at  the back of the car which is increased  in height and width the diffuser is  essentially an exit nozzle for the air  underneath the car and by having a  larger opening the air exiting from  underneath the car is forced to expand  and accelerate to fill the space this  causes the air pressure to drop even  further as Bernoulli's principle tells  us an increase in velocity of a fluid  corresponds with a decrease in air  pressure the diffuser provides a huge  amount of downforce with very little  drag the same can't be said for the rear  wing which was placed high above the car  in 2016  creating a lot of additional drag the  rear wing has dropped down in 2017  reducing its effect on drag as it's  placed in the air that has already been  affected by the structures upstream this  was an effort to maintain the top speed  of the car as drag has increased  dramatically with the widening of the  car the teams have been provided an  additional five kilograms of fuel to  counteract this also as we are halfway  through the 2017 season we are able to  see the effects these changes have made  on f1 without a doubt we have some of  the best-looking caris f1 have seen in a  long time and they are breaking records  again thanks to the additional traction  provided by the wider wheels and  superior aerodynamics but this  additional speed and corners had to  sacrifice some of the exciting  overtaking action as the wider Kerris  are generating a great deal more dirty  air behind them make  it difficult for following carrots to  get enough downforce in corners so have  these changes made f1 more exciting to  watch I'll leave that for you to decide  many of you ask me what software are  used to animate these videos and how I  learned in today's video I use the  combination of Adobe After Effects and  sim scale I thought myself how to use  both by watching videos online these  days you can teach yourself pretty much  any skill online and Skillshare is a  fantastic place to do it with  professional and understandable classes  that follow a clear learning curve  Skillshare is an online learning  community for creators with more than  15,000 classes in graphic design  animation web development music  photography video game design and more  unfortunately there are no engineering  simulation classes there yet  but I am working on creating my own for  Skillshare at this very moment a Premium  Membership begins at about $10 a month  for unlimited access to all courses but  the first 500 people to sign up with  this link will get a 2 month free trial  in those 2 months you could easily learn  the skills you need to start a new hobby  or business just like I did with this  channel so ask yourself right now  what skill have you been putting off  learning what project have you been  dreaming of completing but you aren't  sure if you have the skills to do it  why not start right now and sign up the  skill share using the link below you  have nothing to lose and a valuable life  skill to gain as usual thanks for  watching and thank you to my patreon  supporters I just uploaded a new episode  of show makers my podcast with Sam from  Wendover productions with special guests  from the wisecrack channel with a link  to that and the links to my Twitter  Facebook and Instagram pages are below

